 ## Proof
 
-1.1 Give $S^1$ its one-vertex, one-edge CW structure and $D^2$ its one-vertex, one-cell structure. By the oriented-knot convention [F1], the core circle has the specified positive orientation used for the section generator. The solid tube $S^1\times D^2$ retracts radially to the core circle, whose cellular boundary is zero, so $H_1(S^1\times D^2;\mathbb Z)\cong\mathbb Z$. The product torus $T=S^1\times S^1$ has one $0$-cell, two $1$-cells and one $2$-cell; the two endpoint incidences cancel in degree $1$, and the attaching commutator of the $2$-cell has total signed incidence zero on each $1$-cell. Thus $H_1(T;\mathbb Z)\cong\mathbb Z^2$, with basis the fibre class $[\mu]$ and section class $[\sigma]$. The projection to the first $S^1$ sends $[\mu]$ to $0$ and $[\sigma]$ to $1$. [given, F1, F4, F5, algebra]
